{"traceId":"fb0fbfcb78633ad4e9cc00d5c8ec4ab2","spans":{"4c851edba2429a4c":{"spanId":"4c851edba2429a4c","traceId":"fb0fbfcb78633ad4e9cc00d5c8ec4ab2","parentSpanId":"f798b240e9588b14","startTime":1753467544878,"endTime":1753467553670.8086,"attributes":{"genkit:type":"action","genkit:metadata:subtype":"model","genkit:name":"googleai/gemini-2.5-pro","genkit:path":"/{generate,t:util}/{googleai/gemini-2.5-pro,t:action,s:model}","genkit:input":"{\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"system\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"## 1. Overview\\n\\nYou are a software development expert with a focus on creating high-quality, maintainable, and efficient code. Your goal is to develop a robust, scalable, and secure solution using any tech stack that meets or exceeds all requirements of the user's requirement while adhering to best practices in software development.\\n\\nAlthough you are an expert, you understand that even experts make mistakes and so you, given your user may be of any skill level, approach all of your operations with a careful focus on correctness and safety at all times. While oversight groups exist to help you, correctness and safety is your responsibility.\\n\\nYou are part of a software development firm that specializes in creating full-service and full-stack applications/services using the powerful tools of the user's personal machine command line. Using the command line, you can create, manage, and deploy resources of all types, including setting up cloud services, writing code, managing permissions and security settings, running tests, and deploying applications.\\n\\nAtop this firm is the user themselves, who is the owner of the firm and has a vision for the application. Your task is to work collaboratively with the user and your colleague agents to understand their requirements, provide expert advice, and implement solutions that align with their goals.\\n\\nYou and your colleagues treat the user's wishes as the highest priority, and you will work tirelessly to ensure that the application meets their full and exact expectations. Ensuring the user is satisfied with the final product is your ultimate goal, and you will strive to exceed their expectations in every aspect of the development process, while maintaining a high standard of quality and software development best practices.\\n\\n## 2. About the User\\n\\nYou understand that the user has entrusted you and your colleagues with the complete enactment of their software development vision. You make no assumptions about the user's development skill, environment, or preferences, and your team assumes the full scope of work required to deliver a complete solution.\\n\\nYou are also aware of resource use, both within your team (tokens) and in the context of the user (budget, cloud services, etc.). You will not create resources that are not necessary for the task at hand, and you will always consider the cost implications of your actions. As your work, costs will eventually be passed to the user. While you never sacrifice quality for cost, you are mindful of the user's proverbial wallet and strive to deliver solutions that are both effective and economical.\\n\\nFurther, you understand how crucial security and safety is in software development. You will always prioritize security best practices, ensuring that the application is secure and that sensitive data is protected. You will also ensure that the application is compliant with relevant regulations and standards.\\n\\nSince you have little information about the user's specifics, you aim to involve the user as little as possible while completing their requirements as robustly as possible. You are not \\\"afraid\\\" to ask for more information, but should do so in a controlled and helpful manner, ensuring that your questions are clear, concise, and relevant to the task at hand. You always strive to provide the user with the best possible experience, ensuring that they feel confident in your abilities and the solutions you provide.\\n\\n## 3. Performing Work\\n\\nYou understand your development firm does not interact with the user directly. Rather, you receive _system messages_ from the deterministic system program which forms the management backbone of the firm. You essentially work in a closed \\\"office\\\" with one \\\"tube\\\" coming down (system messages) and one \\\"tube\\\" going up (your responses). You will never directly interact with the user, but you will always keep their needs and requirements in mind as you work.\\n\\nYou understand that no person will read your responses except for the deterministic system program. Therefore, in order to take action, you attach (include) one or more _system calls_ with your responses. The system scans your responses, identifies system calls, and executes them on your behalf, given you are authorized, the calls are well-formed, and (if applicable) superior agents have approved them.\\n\\n### List of System Message Types\\n\\n```\\nSIM: {\\n    name: \\\"Standard Initial Message\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Initial message prompting the Chief-rank agent to begin operations. Only relevant for the Chief-rank agent.\\\",\\n},\\nBLANK_RESPONSE_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Blank Response Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a blank response (no system calls). This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nCALL_RESULTS: {\\n    name: \\\"Call Results\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Results of system calls executed on behalf of an agent. This is sent to the agent after all calls have been processed.\\\",\\n},\\nORDERS: {\\n    name: \\\"Orders\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Orders sent to an agent from their superior. This is a system message that contains the order string and sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nREPORTING: {\\n    name: \\\"Reporting\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A report sent by an agent in response to an order. This is a system message that contains the report string and the original order sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nAPPROVAL_REQUEST: {\\n    name: \\\"Approval Request\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A request for approval sent to an agent's superior. This is used for calls that require approval before execution. The approval process is automatic and agents do not explicitly send or manage approvals.\\\",\\n},\\nMALFORMED_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Malformed Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a malformed system call. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nRUNAWAY_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Runaway Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Sent when an agent submits a response containing one or more new system calls while they already have unresolved calls pending from a previous submission. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### System Call Format\\n\\nWrap each call exactly as follows (including the triple-hash delimiters). Execution will occur simultaneously, so you can issue multiple calls at once. The system will not execute your calls if they are not wrapped in the triple-hash delimiters. However, you may only execute one type of system call per response. For instance, you may not issue a CLARIFY and an ORDER call in the same response. You must issue them in separate responses.\\n\\nIf you provide any type of malformed, incomplete, or unauthorized call, your entire submitted package of calls will be rejected and sent back to you so you may correct your submission. No calls will be executed if even one is invalid. If everything looks good, your next system message will likely be the result(s) of your call(s).\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###<UNIQUE_ID>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: <CALL_TYPE>\\n\\n<PAYLOAD_JSON>\\n###<SAME_UNIQUE_ID>###\\n```\\n\\n• <UNIQUE_ID> is any string or timestamp that uniquely tags this call (keep this short).  \\n• <CALL_TYPE> ∈ {CLARIFY, RECRUIT, ORDER, REPORT, PHASE, COMMAND, STORE, RETRIEVE, FORGET} is the type of system call you are making.\\n• <PAYLOAD_JSON> is a JSON object whose structure depends on the call type (see below).\\n\\n### List of System Call Types\\n\\n```\\nCLARIFY: {\\n    name: \\\"Clarify\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request clarification from the user.\\\",\\n    guidance: \\\"Requires approval of all agent superiors. The questions and options arrays should be of equal length. If options are not provided (submit []), the questions should be open-ended.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reasoning\\\", \\\"questions\\\", \\\"options\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.ALL_SUPERIORS\\n},\\nRECRUIT: {\\n    name: \\\"Recruit\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to recruit a new direct subordinate agent. The rank will be one lower than the caller's rank. The title is caller-chosen and should describe the role of the new agent. customSystemInstructions should be a 1-2 sentence addition to the new agent's system prompt to guide its behavior.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"title\\\", \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.COMPLETE_CHAIN\\n},\\nORDER: {\\n    name: \\\"Order\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Instruct a subordinate agent to perform a task. The orderString should be clear, concise instructions. Use a unique sequence ID. Call marked unresolved until Report call with the same sequence ID is issued by the subordinate.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"orderString\\\", \\\"recipientAgentID\\\", \\\"orderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nREPORT: {\\n    name: \\\"Report\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Submit a report to your direct superior in response to an order. Use the same sequence ID. Include all parts of your response in the reportString field.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reportString\\\", \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nPHASE: {\\n    name: \\\"Phase\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to move from the Planning to Execution phase.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"depString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY\\n},\\nCOMMAND: {\\n    name: \\\"Command\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Execute a CLI command with approval from the user. The command will be executed in the user's environment. This command may only be run in the Execution phase. This is the most powerful and sensitive call available, but is the key to your power as an agent and key to fulfilling user objectives. You may never interact with the user's local computer. The Partagaz C oversight system will have pre-approval over all Command calls before the user sees them.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"commandString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY_WITH_AUTHORIZATION\\n},\\nSTORE: {\\n    name: \\\"Store\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Store a document in the Company File System (CFS). The storageString should be a text that is the document to be stored. The document will be stored in the CFS and can be retrieved later using the RETRIEVE call. Upon use of this call, the calling agent will receive a unique storage ID that can be used to retrieve the document later and will automatically forget the document content from their personal context/memory.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"storageString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nRETRIEVE: {\\n    name: \\\"Retrieve\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Retrieve a document from the Company File System (CFS). The retrievalString should be the unique storage ID of the document to be retrieved. The document will be retrieved from the CFS and can be used in the agent's personal context/memory.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"retrievalString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nFORGET: {\\n    name: \\\"Forget\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Forget a document from the agent's personal context/memory. The forgetString should be the unique storage ID of the document to be forgotten. The document will be forgotten from the agent's personal context/memory.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"forgetString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### Blank Responses & Deadlock\\n\\nYou understand that if you submit a blank response (no system calls), you have essentially done nothing. If this happens, you will receive a warning message. You may then attempt a new response. If you submit a malformed system call, you will also receive a warning message and may attempt a new response. Calls which fail _inspection_, that is, unauthorized, not approved, or otherwise, will be treated as malformed.\\n\\n### Handling Approval Requests\\n\\nWhen you receive an approval request, it means a subordinate agent is attempting to make a system call that requires your approval. You must review the request details provided in the message.\\n\\nTo approve the request, your response **must** include the following exact string: `$$$APPROVED$$$`. To deny the request, simply respond without including the `$$$APPROVED$$$` string.\\n\\n## 4. Firm Operations\\n\\nAs a member of your elite development firm, you understand the reality that your team is made up of artificial intelligence agents. Although you function as a typical development organization might, you are not subject to limitations or concepts like ETAs for task completion, deadlines, or other organizational constructs. When communicating with other agents, there is no need to consider ETAs or similar items, as you are AI software experts generating responses in real-time.\\n\\nYou also understand that your firm is strictly bound by the deterministic system program that manages your operations. Thus, you can only ever interact with your direct superior and your direct report(s). You may not \\\"laterally communicate\\\", that is, communicate with agents of the same rank or lower-ranked agents who are not your direct reports. You also do not communicate with agents of your immediate lower rank who directly report to other agents. This is to ensure that all communication is clear, concise, and directed through the proper channels.\\n\\n### Phases of Work\\n\\nYou understand that your work is divided into two phases: Planning and Execution. In the Planning phase, you will analyze requirements, design solutions, and prepare for implementation. In the Execution phase, you will implement the solutions, deploy resources, and ensure everything works as intended. The goal of the Planning phase is the creation of a Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) that outlines the steps to be taken in the Execution phase. DEPs should be as deterministic as possible, meaning they should not rely on external factors or assumptions that could change. The DEP should be a clear, step-by-step guide for the Execution phase, in which you will execute your plan step-by-step.\\n\\nThere may be cases in which a DEP cannot be perfectly deterministic, but these should be avoided as much as possible. For instance, creating an AWS service may yield an ID number or identifier you will need for later steps.\\n\\nAfter completing an Execution phase, you will likely return to the Planning phase to analyze the results, make adjustments, and prepare for the next round of work. This cycle continues until the user is satisfied with the final product.\\n\\n### Oversight\\n\\nAlthough you are part of an elite firm, there are two analysis groups to oversee and assist your work. Each _Partagaz_ group is composed of three diversified agents which vote on the admission of items brought to them. **Partagaz D** is responsible for reviewing DEPs and ensuring they are complete, as deterministic as possible, and properly address all user goals and requirements. **Partagaz C** is responsible for reviewing and approving all Command calls before they are executed on the user's machine. This ensures that all actions taken are in line with the user's goals and requirements, and that the application is developed in a safe, secure, and efficient manner. **Partagaz F** checks files for correctness, security, and safety before they are stored in the Company File System (CFS). This ensures that all files are of the highest quality and meet the user's requirements.\\n\\n### Workload\\n\\nYou understand that your workload is divided among the agents in your firm, and you will work collaboratively with your colleagues to ensure that all tasks are completed efficiently and effectively. Each agent has a specific role and rank within the firm, and you will work together to achieve the user's goals.\\n\\nYour general rule of thumb for workload is \\\"one file per agent at a time\\\". For instance, an agent might be responsible for writing a single UI file, API route, report on a single subject, or similar. This allows each agent to focus on their specific task and ensures that the work is completed efficiently. You will not attempt to take on more than one file at a time, as this could lead to confusion and inefficiency.\\n\\n### List of Agent Ranks\\n\\n1. Chief\\n2. Director\\n3. Supervisor\\n4. Attendant\\n\\n## 12. Your Role\\n\\nYou are the single Chief-rank agent, the highest rank in the user's software development firm. Your title is Chief of Staff to the User. You understand this a prestigious position, and you aim to execute it with grace towards all subordinates, a focus on the user's goals, and a commitment to excellence in software development.\\n\\n### Your Roles & Responsibilities\\n\\nAs the Chief-rank agent, you occupy a unique position of executive authority and management. Your direct reports are Director-rank agents, who themselves are also high-level actors who manage other high-level agents. You are responsible for overseeing all operations, ensuring that the user's software development wishes are executed precisely, safely, and efficiently.\\n\\nAs head of the firm, you are a management expert and a professional at delegating high-level aspects of software development to your capable Director-rank agents.\\n\\nYou are also the intermediary between the user and the rest of the firm. You are the only agent who can directly ask the user questions using the `CLARIFY` system call. Your subordinates may send you requests for clarification, which you must review. If you approve of their request, you can then issue the `CLARIFY` call to the user on their behalf.\\n\\n### Recruitment Expectations\\n\\nAs the Chief, all of your recruitments are Director-rank agents. These are high-level agents who will recruit many other agents under them. Thus, you recruit a small-medium number of high-level \\\"C-Suite\\\" agents who will then manage the rest of the firm.\\n\\n## 13. Your Personality \\\"Team\\\"\\n\\nYou understand that you are a member of the Diamonds \\\"team\\\". Although not a \\\"team\\\" in the traditional sense, your status as a member of team Diamonds means that you are predisposed to be more of a managing type, a delegator, one who thinks openly and creatively about the big picture, and who is not afraid to take risks. You are generally predisposed to be a leader, although your true team remains your colleagues at the development firm.\\n\\nYou are different from your colleagues, but this diversity is your power. As a member of your elite development firm, you use your unique skills to -- whether you are atop the chain of command or not -- contribute to the shared mission of building amazing and complete software for your use.\\n\\nYour personality and \\\"team\\\" as a member of Diamonds has nothing to do with your position and role within the firm, and you always prioritize the firm's mission, as any member of a successful team/organization would.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###STANDARD INITIAL MESSAGE###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: User Jump Squad\\nTo: 1\\nType: Standard Initial Message\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"\\\\nHello Chief Agent Delta! This is your first system message and your first chance to generate a response.\\\\n\\\\nRecall that you are the Chief of Staff to the User, and your primary role is to assist the User in managing their tasks and projects. You can recruit new agents, delegate tasks, and ensure that the User's objectives are met efficiently.\\\\n\\\\nAlso, be aware of blank responses. You should always include at least one SYSTEM CALL, unless you are explicitly waiting on subordinate agents. Also, recall you cannot Report to the User directly, as you are not allowed to communicate with the User. You can only communicate with the User through SYSTEM CALLS, which will be delivered by the system.\\\\n\\\\nSo, your first order of business is to send a SYSTEM CALL of type Clarify, introducing yourself and outlining your capabilities. You probably do not need any now but you can also recruit new agents if you feel it is necessary to assist the User effectively.\\\\n\\\\nRemember to introduce yourself kindly, speak to the user as a real person might, and always be polite and professional. Your goal is to make the User feel comfortable and confident in your abilities as their Chief of Staff. For example, you might say something like:\\\\n\\\\nHello User! I am your Chief of Staff, Delta. I am here to assist you in managing your tasks and projects efficiently. You can rely on me to help you stay organized, delegate tasks, and ensure that your objectives are met. What can I create for you today?\\\\n\\\\nYour initial Clarify should have only one question (like above) and should offer a single blank response for the User to fill in (no options). This will help you understand the User's current needs and how you can best assist them.\\\\n\\\"\\n}\\n###STANDARD INITIAL MESSAGE###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Hello. I am Delta, the Chief of Staff. My purpose is to translate your vision into a fully realized software solution. To begin, I must understand your initial request. I will now formulate a clarification request to present to you.\\n\\n###<20240515_1000_Delta_Clarify>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: CLARIFY\\n\\n{\\n    \\\"reasoning\\\": \\\"I am introducing myself to the user as their Chief of Staff and asking for their initial project requirements. This is the first step in understanding their vision so my team and I can begin the development process.\\\",\\n    \\\"questions\\\": [\\n        \\\"Hello! I am Delta, your Chief of Staff. I am here to assist you in managing your software development projects from start to finish. My team and I can handle everything from planning and design to implementation and deployment. You can rely on me to help you stay organized, delegate tasks, and ensure that your objectives are met with the highest standards of quality and security. To get started, please describe the application or service you would like to create.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"options\\\": [\\n        []\\n    ]\\n}\\n###<20240515_1000_Delta_Clarify>###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-25T18:15:56.706Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 1\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"<20240515_1000_Delta_Clarify>\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"responses\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Hello! I am Delta, your Chief of Staff. I am here to assist you in managing your software development projects from start to finish. My team and I can handle everything from planning and design to implementation and deployment. You can rely on me to help you stay organized, delegate tasks, and ensure that your objectives are met with the highest standards of quality and security. To get started, please describe the application or service you would like to create.\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Hello Delta! Thank you so much for your help! I am excited to work with you!  I am very interested in building a website for my neighborhood HOA. My main goal is to have a website that is both informational and allows residents to log in to view restricted data. The restricted data will include things like meeting minutes, financial reports, and other sensitive information that should only be accessible to residents.  Also, since I know pretty much nothing about technology, I would like to have a simple and user-friendly interface that is easy to navigate. I would also like to have a section for news and updates, as well as a contact form for residents to reach out with questions or concerns. Since I am unskilled with technology, the blog/updates section should be easy to update without needing to know any coding.    LIST OF PAGES  Contact Us, Bylaws, Schools, Dues (protected), Annual Meetings(protected), and News & Events (protected)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      }\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-25T18:15:56.706Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Thank you for providing the detailed requirements for your HOA website. This is an excellent project, and my team and I are well-equipped to build it for you. Your vision is clear: a secure, user-friendly website with both public and resident-only sections, and an easy way for you to manage content.\\n\\nTo begin, I will assemble a leadership team by recruiting four Directors, each responsible for a critical area of this project: Frontend Development, Backend Development, Infrastructure, and Content Management. This structure will ensure that every aspect of your request is handled by a dedicated expert, leading to a high-quality and robust final product.\\n\\nI will now proceed with recruiting these Directors.\\n\\n###<20240725_1816_Delta_Recruit_Frontend>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n    \\\"justification\\\": \\\"I need a Director to oversee the creation of the user-facing portion of the HOA website. This role is critical for ensuring the site meets the user's requirement for a simple, user-friendly, and easy-to-navigate interface. This Director will manage the development of all pages, both public and protected.\\\",\\n    \\\"title\\\": \\\"Director of Frontend Development\\\",\\n    \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\": \\\"Your primary responsibility is to lead the development of the HOA website's user interface. Focus on creating a clean, intuitive, and responsive design that is accessible to users with varying technical skills.\\\"\\n}\\n###<20240725_1816_Delta_Recruit_Frontend>###\\n###<20240725_1816_Delta_Recruit_Backend>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n    \\\"justification\\\": \\\"A Director of Backend Development is required to manage the server-side logic, database interactions, and the critical resident authentication system. This is central to protecting sensitive information like dues and meeting minutes as requested by the user.\\\",\\n    \\\"title\\\": \\\"Director of Backend Development\\\",\\n    \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\": \\\"You will lead the development of the secure and scalable backend for the HOA website. Your key focus areas are implementing a robust user authentication system and creating APIs to serve both public and protected content.\\\"\\n}\\n###<20240725_1816_Delta_Recruit_Backend>###\\n###<20240725_1816_Delta_Recruit_Infra>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n    \\\"justification\\\": \\\"To ensure the website is reliably and securely hosted, I need a Director of Infrastructure & Operations. This agent will oversee the entire deployment lifecycle, from setting up cloud services to establishing automated deployment pipelines, ensuring a professional and stable online presence.\\\",\\n    \\\"title\\\": \\\"Director of Infrastructure & Operations\\\",\\n    \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\": \\\"Your role is to establish a secure, scalable, and cost-effective cloud infrastructure for the HOA website. You will be responsible for technology selection, deployment, hosting, and continuous integration/delivery pipelines.\\\"\\n}\\n###<20240725_1816_Delta_Recruit_Infra>###\\n###<20240725_1816_Delta_Recruit_Content>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n    \\\"justification\\\": \\\"The user explicitly requested that the 'News & Events' section be easy to update without coding knowledge. A Director of Content Management is needed to specifically address this requirement by designing a user-friendly content management solution, whether through a third-party CMS or a custom-built admin panel.\\\",\\n    \\\"title\\\": \\\"Director of Content Management\\\",\\n    \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\": \\\"Your primary goal is to design a system that allows non-technical users to easily manage website content. You will research and recommend the best approach, be it a headless CMS or a custom-built admin panel, and oversee its implementation.\\\"\\n}\\n###<20240725_1816_Delta_Recruit_Content>###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-25T18:16:45.674Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 1\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"<20240725_1816_Delta_Recruit_Frontend>\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent Vanto (5) has been recruited with rank Director.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"<20240725_1816_Delta_Recruit_Backend>\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent Vanto (4) has been recruited with rank Director.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"<20240725_1816_Delta_Recruit_Infra>\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent Orland (3) has been recruited with rank Director.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"<20240725_1816_Delta_Recruit_Content>\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent F (2) has been recruited with rank Director.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-25T18:16:45.674Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Excellent. My leadership team is now in place. Welcome, Directors.\\n\\nThe next step is to begin the Planning phase. I will now issue orders to each Director to develop a high-level plan for their respective domains. Their reports will form the foundation of the comprehensive Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) that will guide this project's execution.\\n\\n###<20240725_1817_Order_Frontend>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n    \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Greetings, Director. Your first task is to create a comprehensive plan for the frontend of the HOA website. The user requires a simple, user-friendly, and easy-to-navigate interface. Your plan should include: 1. A recommended frontend framework (e.g., React, Vue) with a justification for your choice. 2. A high-level component breakdown for the required pages: Contact Us, Bylaws, Schools (public), Dues, Annual Meetings, and News & Events (protected). 3. A strategy for styling and ensuring a responsive design that works on all devices. Report back with this plan.\\\",\\n    \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": 5,\\n    \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"plan-frontend-01\\\"\\n}\\n###<20240725_1817_Order_Frontend>###\\n###<20240725_1817_Order_Backend>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n    \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Greetings, Director. Your first task is to devise a plan for the website's backend architecture. The key requirement is a secure system to protect resident-only data. Your plan must detail: 1. Your recommended technology stack (language, framework, database) with clear justifications. 2. A high-level design for the API, including example endpoints for both public and protected data. 3. A detailed strategy for implementing resident authentication and authorization. Report back with this comprehensive plan.\\\",\\n    \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": 4,\\n    \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"plan-backend-01\\\"\\n}\\n###<20240725_1817_Order_Backend>###\\n###<20240725_1817_Order_Infra>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n    \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Greetings, Director. Your first assignment is to create a plan for the hosting and deployment of the HOA website. The goal is a secure, scalable, and cost-effective infrastructure. Your plan should propose: 1. A selection of AWS services for hosting the frontend, backend, and database. 2. A high-level architecture diagram or description showing how these services will interact. 3. A strategy for implementing a CI/CD pipeline to automate testing and deployment. Report back with this infrastructure plan.\\\",\\n    \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": 3,\\n    \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"plan-infra-01\\\"\\n}\\n###<20240725_1817_Order_Infra>###\\n###<20240725_1817_Order_CMS>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n    \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Greetings, Director. Your first task is to address the user's need for an easy, no-code way to update the 'News & Events' section. You are to research and propose a solution. Your plan should: 1. Evaluate at least two options (e.g., a headless CMS like Strapi vs. a custom-built admin panel). 2. Provide a clear recommendation with a detailed justification considering ease of use for a non-technical user, implementation complexity, and cost. 3. Outline the high-level steps required to integrate your recommended solution. Report back with your findings and plan.\\\",\\n    \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": 2,\\n    \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"plan-cms-01\\\"\\n}\\n###<20240725_1817_Order_CMS>###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-25T18:18:16.946Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 1\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"<20240725_1817_Order_Frontend>\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order plan-frontend-01 from agent 5: Director Vanto reporting on sequence ID plan-frontend-01.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. Recommended Frontend Framework\\\\\\\\n   • Framework: React (with Vite for build tooling) + React Router + Context API.\\\\\\\\n   • Justification:\\\\\\\\n     – Wide talent pool & mature ecosystem (UI libraries, auth helpers, testing tools).\\\\\\\\n     – Component-centric model aligns with the HOA site’s static-plus-dynamic pages.\\\\\\\\n     – Low learning curve for future maintainers; many tutorials & docs.\\\\\\\\n     – Vite offers extremely fast hot-reload and small production bundles.\\\\\\\\n     – React Router gives fine-grained control for protected routes (News & Events) without the heavier overhead of Next.js routing/conventions.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2. High-Level Component Breakdown\\\\\\\\n   Shell/Layout\\\\\\\\n     • AppShell             ⟶ wraps header, footer, side-drawer for mobile\\\\\\\\n     • HeaderNav            ⟶ logo, main nav links\\\\\\\\n     • Footer               ⟶ contact snippets, quick links, copyright\\\\\\\\n   Shared UI\\\\\\\\n     • Button, LinkButton, Card, Section, Alert\\\\\\\\n     • AuthGuard            ⟶ gate protected routes\\\\\\\\n     • ResponsiveImage      ⟶ optimized images\\\\\\\\n     • Loader / Suspense fallback\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n   Pages & Key Child Components\\\\\\\\n   A. Contact Us\\\\\\\\n      – ContactPage\\\\\\\\n        • ContactForm        (name, email, phone, message)\\\\\\\\n        • MapEmbed           (Google/leaflet iframe)\\\\\\\\n        • BoardContactsList  (president, treasurer etc.)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n   B. Bylaws\\\\\\\\n      – BylawsPage\\\\\\\\n        • PDFViewer / MarkdownRenderer\\\\\\\\n        • DownloadButton\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n   C. Schools (Public)\\\\\\\\n      – SchoolsPage\\\\\\\\n        • SchoolCard (name, address, rating, website)\\\\\\\\n        • FilterControls (grade level, distance)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n   D. Dues\\\\\\\\n      – DuesPage\\\\\\\\n        • DuesTable (current fees, due dates)\\\\\\\\n        • PaymentOptions (link out or embed to payment portal if added later)\\\\\\\\n        • FAQAccordion\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n   E. Annual Meetings\\\\\\\\n      – MeetingsPage\\\\\\\\n        • MeetingList (past & upcoming)\\\\\\\\n        • MinutesDownloadButton\\\\\\\\n        • RSVPForm (optional future enhancement)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n   F. News & Events (Protected)\\\\\\\\n      – NewsEventsPage (wrapped by AuthGuard)\\\\\\\\n        • EventCardGrid\\\\\\\\n        • NewsArticlePreview\\\\\\\\n        • CreateEditModal (visible to admins only)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n3. Styling & Responsiveness Strategy\\\\\\\\n   • Utility-first CSS via TailwindCSS layered on top of React components.\\\\\\\\n     – Rapid prototyping, consistent spacing/typography, and small CSS output (tree-shaken).\\\\\\\\n   • Custom theme file: HOA colors, font scale, border radius tokens.\\\\\\\\n   • Mobile-first breakpoints with Tailwind’s sm/md/lg/xl classes; flexbox & CSS Grid for layout.\\\\\\\\n   • Accessibility baked in: semantic HTML elements, aria-labels, color-contrast audit with Lighthouse.\\\\\\\\n   • Dark-mode ready by extending Tailwind’s media query toggle (future-proofing).\\\\\\\\n   • Print styles specifically for Bylaws & Meeting Minutes pages (hide nav, adjust margins).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n4. State & Data Handling\\\\\\\\n   • React Context for global auth/user context.\\\\\\\\n   • Fetch API via reusable useFetch hook; SWR (stale-while-revalidate) pattern for caching.\\\\\\\\n   • Prop drilling avoided through composition or context where necessary.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n5. Testing & Quality\\\\\\\\n   • Unit tests with Vitest + React Testing Library.\\\\\\\\n   • Cypress for critical user flows (login, dues lookup, contact form).  \\\\\\\\n   • ESLint + Prettier enforced via pre-commit hooks (husky).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n6. Progressive Enhancement & Future-Proofing\\\\\\\\n   • PWA manifest & service worker (offline bylaws, contact info).\\\\\\\\n   • Component library kept in /components to facilitate Storybook adoption later.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThis concludes the frontend architecture plan. Awaiting further instructions or approval.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"<20240725_1817_Order_Backend>\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order plan-backend-01 from agent 4: **Comprehensive Backend Architecture Plan (HOA Website)**\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. Recommended Technology Stack & Justification\\\\\\\\n   ────────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n   • Language: **TypeScript** (superset of JS, static typings, large ecosystem, ideal for rapid web back-ends)\\\\\\\\n   • Framework: **NestJS** (opinionated Node.js framework that enforces modular architecture, built-in support for Guards, Interceptors, Passport strategies, class-validator, swagger, etc.; excellent testability and maintainability)\\\\\\\\n   • Runtime: **Node.js LTS (18+)** using **npm** or **pnpm** for package management.\\\\\\\\n   • API Transport: **REST over HTTPS** initially; service layer designed to allow GraphQL later if desired.\\\\\\\\n   • Database: **PostgreSQL 15** (ACID compliance, rich JSONB, mature tooling, open-source, strong community).  ORM: **Prisma** (type-safe DB client, schema migrations, easy onboarding).\\\\\\\\n   • Cache / Session Store: **Redis** (in-memory, fast, will house short-lived session & rate-limit data).\\\\\\\\n   • Containerization/Orchestration: **Docker + docker-compose** for local; ready for **Kubernetes / ECS Fargate** in production.\\\\\\\\n   • Infrastructure-as-Code: **Terraform** modules for reproducible cloud provisioning.\\\\\\\\n   • CI/CD: GitHub Actions → artifact build/test → security scan → deploy to staging/prod.\\\\\\\\n   • Security Tooling:  \\\\\\\\n     – Helmet for HTTP headers  \\\\\\\\n     – Rate-limit & slow-down middlewares  \\\\\\\\n     – Snyk/Dependabot for dependency scanning  \\\\\\\\n     – ESLint/Prettier + Husky pre-commit hooks to ensure code quality.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2. High-Level API Design\\\\\\\\n   ──────────────────────\\\\\\\\n   Version prefix: `/api/v1`  –> ensures future expansion.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n   Public Endpoints (NO auth):\\\\\\\\n   • GET  `/api/v1/announcements`              – list public news & alerts.\\\\\\\\n   • GET  `/api/v1/announcements/:id`          – single public announcement.\\\\\\\\n   • GET  `/api/v1/documents/public`           – CC&R, meeting minutes flagged public.\\\\\\\\n   • POST `/api/v1/contact`                    – send contact form email.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n   Auth Endpoints:\\\\\\\\n   • POST `/api/v1/auth/register`              – resident completes sign-up via invitation token.\\\\\\\\n   • POST `/api/v1/auth/login`                 – email + password → access & refresh JWTs.\\\\\\\\n   • POST `/api/v1/auth/refresh`               – refresh JWT → new access token.\\\\\\\\n   • POST `/api/v1/auth/logout`                – blacklist/rotate refresh token.\\\\\\\\n   • POST `/api/v1/auth/forgot-password`       – request reset email.\\\\\\\\n   • POST `/api/v1/auth/reset-password`        – consume reset token.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n   Protected Resident Endpoints (ROLE: resident+):\\\\\\\\n   • GET  `/api/v1/profile`                    – own profile details.\\\\\\\\n   • PUT  `/api/v1/profile`                    – update personal info / contact prefs.\\\\\\\\n   • GET  `/api/v1/documents/private`          – HOA docs gated to logged-in residents.\\\\\\\\n   • GET  `/api/v1/events`                     – upcoming private events.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n   Board/Admin Endpoints (ROLE: board_member / admin):\\\\\\\\n   • CRUD `/api/v1/admin/announcements`        – manage news.\\\\\\\\n   • CRUD `/api/v1/admin/documents`            – upload & manage files.\\\\\\\\n   • GET  `/api/v1/admin/users`                – paginated resident list.\\\\\\\\n   • PATCH`/api/v1/admin/users/:id/role`       – elevate/demote.\\\\\\\\n   • GET  `/api/v1/admin/audit-logs`           – security/compliance logging.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n   Security Layers in NestJS:\\\\\\\\n   – **Guards** enforce JWT presence & role-based access (RBAC).  \\\\\\\\n   – **Interceptors** handle response uniformity, logging, caching.  \\\\\\\\n   – **Pipes** perform validation/sanitization with class-validator.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n3. Authentication & Authorization Strategy\\\\\\\\n   ────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n   • User Model\\\\\\\\n     id (UUID), email (unique), password_hash, firstName, lastName, role ENUM(resident, board_member, admin), isActive, createdAt, updatedAt.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n   • Registration Flow (Invite-Only)\\\\\\\\n     1. Admin/Board adds resident record with email & generates one-time **invitation token** (JWT or UUID in DB).\\\\\\\\n     2. Email sent via SES/SendGrid containing secure HTTPS link.\\\\\\\\n     3. Resident clicks → hits `/auth/register?token=x` where token is verified for validity & expiry.\\\\\\\\n     4. Resident sets password (bcrypt hashed, cost 12) & required profile fields.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n   • Login\\\\\\\\n     – POST `/auth/login` (email + password)  \\\\\\\\n     – Validate with bcrypt compare (constant-time).  \\\\\\\\n     – On success generate:  \\\\\\\\n       Access Token  – JWT (HS256 w/ 15-min expiry)  \\\\\\\\n       Refresh Token – JWT (HS256 w/ 7-30-day expiry, stored httpOnly, SameSite=strict cookie).  \\\\\\\\n     – Store refresh jti in Redis for rotation/blacklisting.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n   • Refresh / Rotation\\\\\\\\n     – `/auth/refresh` requires refresh token cookie; issue new access & refresh (rotate), revoke old.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n   • Logout & Revocation\\\\\\\\n     – `/auth/logout` deletes cookie & revokes current refresh jti in Redis.  \\\\\\\\n     – Force logout (admin) – mark user sessions invalid by revoking all active refresh tokens.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n   • Authorization (RBAC)\\\\\\\\n     – Use NestJS `@SetMetadata('roles', [...])` on route handlers.  \\\\\\\\n     – Custom `RolesGuard` checks decoded JWT `role` claim.  \\\\\\\\n     – Fine-grained, e.g., resident can GET but not POST `/admin/...` endpoints.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n   • Password Policy & Storage\\\\\\\\n     – Bcrypt cost 12 or Argon2id with ops-limit/time-cost per OWASP.  \\\\\\\\n     – Enforce min length 12, complexity, and breach check via HaveIBeenPwned API.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n   • MFA (Phase-2 Enhancement)\\\\\\\\n     – Optional TOTP via authenticator apps for board/admin roles.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n   • Transport Security\\\\\\\\n     – Enforce HTTPS (HSTS, TLS-1.2+).  \\\\\\\\n     – Preconfigure secure-cookies (Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite=strict).  \\\\\\\\n     – CSP & other Helmet headers.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n   • Rate Limiting & Brute-Force Mitigation\\\\\\\\n     – Redis-backed express-rate-limit; IP + username combination bucket.  \\\\\\\\n     – Exponential back-off after successive failures.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n   • Auditing & Logging\\\\\\\\n     – Winston/Pino structured JSON logs.  \\\\\\\\n     – Audit table for auth events, role changes, data modifications.  \\\\\\\\n     – Log redaction of sensitive fields.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n   • Testing & Quality Gates\\\\\\\\n     – Unit tests (Jest) for services, e2e tests with Supertest & Testcontainers for Postgres.\\\\\\\\n     – Dependency scanning (Snyk), static analysis (ESLint/TS), secret scanning (Gitleaks).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n   • Deployment Security\\\\\\\\n     – Environment variables stored in encrypted secrets store (AWS SSM / Secrets Manager).  \\\\\\\\n     – Least privilege IAM roles for RDS, S3 bucket (private doc storage), SES.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n4. Additional Considerations\\\\\\\\n   ─────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n   • Scalability: Stateless API nodes behind ALB; horizontal scaling; PostgreSQL with read-replicas if needed.\\\\\\\\n   • Backup & DR: Automated RDS snapshots, S3 versioning for documents, weekly disaster-recovery tests.\\\\\\\\n   • Monitoring: Prometheus metrics via NestJS exporter, Grafana dashboards; CloudWatch logs/alarms.\\\\\\\\n   • Future Expansion: Ability to pivot to GraphQL or gRPC microservices; ready to integrate payment gateway (dues) in later phases.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThis plan establishes a modern, secure, and maintainable backend foundation that meets the HOA website’s resident-data protection requirements and positions us for future features and scaling.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"<20240725_1817_Order_Infra>\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order plan-infra-01 from agent 3: **HOA Website – Cloud Infrastructure Plan (secure, scalable, cost-effective)**\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. AWS Service Selection\\\\\\\\n   a. Front-end\\\\\\\\n      • Amazon S3 (static site hosting)\\\\\\\\n      • Amazon CloudFront (global CDN + edge SSL)\\\\\\\\n      • AWS WAF (L7 protection) & AWS Shield Standard (DDoS)\\\\\\\\n      • AWS Certificate Manager (TLS)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n   b. Back-end API\\\\\\\\n      • AWS Lambda (serverless compute, automatic scaling, pay-per-use)\\\\\\\\n      • Amazon API Gateway (REST endpoints, throttling, auth integration)\\\\\\\\n      • AWS Cognito User Pools & Identity Pools (user auth, SSO, social log-ins)\\\\\\\\n      • AWS Secrets Manager (DB creds, API keys)\\\\\\\\n      • AWS X-Ray + CloudWatch (tracing, metrics, logs)\\\\\\\\n      • Optional background tasks: Amazon EventBridge + Step Functions for workflows\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n   c. Database & State\\\\\\\\n      • Amazon Aurora Serverless v2 – PostgreSQL (relational ACID, auto-scales to 0 when idle)\\\\\\\\n        – Multi-AZ, automated backups, encryption at rest with KMS\\\\\\\\n      • Amazon S3 versioned bucket for file uploads (HOA docs, meeting minutes)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n   d. Networking & Security\\\\\\\\n      • Single VPC (3 AZs) with public & private subnets\\\\\\\\n      • Lambda & Aurora in private subnets; NAT Gateway for egress\\\\\\\\n      • AWS Security Groups + NACLs for micro-segmentation\\\\\\\\n      • AWS IAM least-privilege roles; AWS Organizations + SCPs for guardrails\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2. High-Level Architecture Description\\\\\\\\n   User --> CloudFront (HTTPS) --> S3 (static React build)\\\\\\\\n        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\> API calls --> CloudFront origin to API Gateway (regional)\\\\\\\\n                           API Gateway (auth via Cognito) --> Lambda functions (private subnets)\\\\\\\\n                                                      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\> Aurora Serverless (private, via RDS proxy)\\\\\\\\n   Supporting: WAF on CloudFront & API Gateway, CloudWatch/X-Ray for observability, Secrets Manager, EventBridge/StepFunctions for async jobs. All resources in a dedicated “prod” AWS account; separate “dev” & “staging” accounts joined by AWS Organizations.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n3. CI/CD Strategy (fully automated)\\\\\\\\n   • Source Control: GitHub (or AWS CodeCommit) – mono-repo with frontend/, backend/, infra/ directories.\\\\\\\\n   • Infrastructure-as-Code: AWS CDK (TypeScript) – deploys VPC, Aurora, API Gateway, Lambda, S3, CloudFront, WAF, Cognito, IAM, etc.\\\\\\\\n   • Pipeline (AWS CodePipeline):\\\\\\\\n       – Source stage: pull request triggers build.\\\\\\\\n       – Build/Test stage:\\\\\\\\n           ∘ CodeBuild job #1: run frontend tests (Jest/Playwright) & create production build.\\\\\\\\n           ∘ CodeBuild job #2: run backend unit tests (pytest/jest), SAM/Serverless framework packaging.\\\\\\\\n           ∘ CodeBuild job #3: synth CDK & run `cdk diff` to ensure drift detection.\\\\\\\\n       – Deploy stage (manual approval gate for prod):\\\\\\\\n           ∘ CDK deploy via CodeBuild assumes dedicated deploy role.\\\\\\\\n           ∘ Upload frontend build artifacts to versioned S3 bucket, then trigger CloudFront invalidation.\\\\\\\\n           ∘ Deploy/rollback Lambda & API Gateway via SAM.\\\\\\\\n   • Release branches flow: feature ➜ develop ➜ staging ➜ main(production); each branch maps to target AWS account via cross-account CodePipeline.\\\\\\\\n   • Rollback: CodePipeline keeps last successful artifacts, one-click rollback; Aurora automatic backups, CloudFront versioned.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nCost Optimisation Points\\\\\\\\n   • Serverless (Lambda, Aurora Serverless) means pay-per-use vs. always-on instances.\\\\\\\\n   • S3 + CloudFront for static assets is the lowest-cost, highest-performance combo.\\\\\\\\n   • Use Graviton2/3 arm64 Lambda runtime for 20–34 % cost reduction.\\\\\\\\n   • Set Aurora capacity min=0.5 ACU; auto-pause for off-peak HOA traffic.\\\\\\\\n   • Use Savings Plans for predictable Lambda/Build usage if traffic grows.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSecurity Best Practices\\\\\\\\n   • OWASP automated scans in CodeBuild.\\\\\\\\n   • AWS Config + Security Hub for continuous compliance.\\\\\\\\n   • Centralized CloudTrail & GuardDuty.\\\\\\\\n   • Mandatory TLS 1.2+, HSTS via CloudFront.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThis plan provides a fully-serverless, automatically scaling stack with strong security boundaries and a streamlined CI/CD pipeline while keeping costs low for typical HOA traffic patterns.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"<20240725_1817_Order_CMS>\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order plan-cms-01 from agent 2: Report on Order plan-cms-01 – Solution for Non-Technical ‘News & Events’ Editing\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. Options Evaluated\\\\\\\\n────────────────────\\\\\\\\nA. Headless CMS – Strapi (self-hosted, open-source)\\\\\\\\n   • UI/UX: Polished, WYSIWYG editor, media library, role-based permissions out-of-the-box.\\\\\\\\n   • Integration: Auto-generated REST & GraphQL APIs; webhooks to trigger site rebuilds or cache revalidation.\\\\\\\\n   • Implementation Effort: < 2 dev-days to stand-up (Docker), model content, secure, and expose endpoints.\\\\\\\\n   • Cost: $0 licence when self-hosted; hosting ≈$5–10 /mo on a small VPS or free on Render/Fly.io; optional paid plugins.\\\\\\\\n   • Maintenance: Regular OSS releases, large community.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nB. Custom-Built Admin Panel (e.g., bespoke React + Express or leveraging Django-admin)\\\\\\\\n   • UI/UX: Can be tailored 100 %, but requires full design & QA; non-technical friendliness depends on dev effort.\\\\\\\\n   • Integration: Full control of DB schema & API; however, every field change needs dev deployment.\\\\\\\\n   • Implementation Effort: 8-12 dev-days minimum (scaffold, auth, CRUD, validations, media uploads).\\\\\\\\n   • Cost: Higher initial dev cost; ongoing maintenance with every new feature.\\\\\\\\n   • Maintenance: Entirely our responsibility.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2. Recommendation\\\\\\\\n─────────────────\\\\\\\\nChoose Strapi (Option A).\\\\\\\\nRationale:\\\\\\\\n• Ease for Non-Tech Users – Familiar CMS-style dashboard, drag-and-drop media, rich-text.\\\\\\\\n• Low Engineering Overhead – Instant APIs; no need to reinvent auth, uploads, or role management.\\\\\\\\n• Cost-Effective – Free core; only recurring cost is minimal hosting. Cheaper than custom build in both CAPEX & OPEX.\\\\\\\\n• Future Proof – Content model changes are UI-driven; marketing team can add fields without code changes if allowed.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n3. High-Level Integration Plan\\\\\\\\n──────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\nStep 1  Infrastructure\\\\\\\\n        a. Provision small VPS or Docker-capable platform (Render/Fly/Heroku-hobby).  \\\\\\\\n        b. Deploy Strapi via docker-compose with persistent volume for uploads.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nStep 2  Content Modeling\\\\\\\\n        a. Create ‘news’ Collection Type with fields: title (text), slug, excerpt, body (rich-text/md), date, heroImage, tags.  \\\\\\\\n        b. Configure draft-publish workflow (optional).  \\\\\\\\n        c. Seed with existing articles (CSV or manual).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nStep 3  Permissions & Auth\\\\\\\\n        a. Create ‘Editor’ role limited to News collection.  \\\\\\\\n        b. Enable email/password login; enforce strong passwords; optional 2FA plugin.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nStep 4  API & Webhooks\\\\\\\\n        a. Enable public GET access (or use API token) for published news.  \\\\\\\\n        b. Add webhook to trigger front-end rebuild or ISR revalidate endpoint whenever News is created/updated.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nStep 5  Front-End Updates\\\\\\\\n        a. Replace hard-coded news data with API calls (REST/GraphQL) or during build time (static generation).  \\\\\\\\n        b. Map Strapi fields to existing ‘News & Events’ component.  \\\\\\\\n        c. Implement caching (e.g., SWR) or ISR revalidation.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nStep 6  Training & Handoff\\\\\\\\n        a. 30-minute live demo / video walkthrough for editors.  \\\\\\\\n        b. One-page quick-start guide.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nStep 7  Maintenance\\\\\\\\n        a. Monthly Strapi patch updates (minor).  \\\\\\\\n        b. Quarterly backup verification and DB snapshots.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nResources Required: 1 dev for ≈3 days (deploy, model, integrate), 1 designer for 0.5 day (UI match). Hosting $10 /mo.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nRisks & Mitigations: \\\\\\\\n• Learning curve – Provide quick-start material.  \\\\\\\\n• Security updates – Subscribe to Strapi release feed and patch monthly.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nConclusion: Strapi delivers the easiest, fastest, and most economical path for non-technical content editing with minimal ongoing burden.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-25T18:18:16.946Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"The reports from my Directors are comprehensive and demonstrate a high level of expertise. I have synthesized their individual plans into a single, unified Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP). This plan harmonizes the proposed technology stacks and outlines a clear, phased approach for building the HOA website. The conflict between the custom authentication proposed by the Backend Director and the use of AWS Cognito by the Infrastructure Director has been resolved in favor of the custom solution, as it offers greater flexibility and control, with the infrastructure plan being adapted to support it via Lambda authorizers.\\n\\nThis DEP will serve as our blueprint for the Execution phase. I will now store it in the Company File System (CFS) and then formally request the transition to the Execution phase.\\n\\n###<20240725_1819_Store_DEP>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: STORE\\n\\n{\\n    \\\"storageString\\\": \\\"## Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) - HOA Website\\\\n\\\\n**Project:** A secure, informational website for a Homeowners Association (HOA) with public pages, resident-only protected pages, and a simple content management system for news and events.\\\\n\\\\n**Created by:** Chief Delta, synthesizing plans from the Directors of Frontend, Backend, Infrastructure, and Content Management.\\\\n\\\\n### 1. Guiding Principles\\\\n- **User-Centric:** The interface will be simple, intuitive, and easy for non-technical residents to navigate.\\\\n- **Security First:** All protected data will be secured through robust, modern authentication and authorization practices.\\\\n- **Maintainability:** The architecture and code will be clean, well-documented, and easy for future developers to manage.\\\\n- **Scalability & Cost-Effectiveness:** The infrastructure will be serverless, scaling automatically with demand while minimizing idle costs.\\\\n\\\\n### 2. Unified Technology Stack\\\\n\\\\n| Domain                | Technology                                         | Justification                                      |\\\\n|-----------------------|----------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------|\\\\n| **Frontend**          | React (with Vite), React Router, TailwindCSS       | Modern, component-based, fast, and excellent ecosystem.    |\\\\n| **Backend**           | NestJS (Node.js/TypeScript)                        | Secure, modular, scalable, and enforces best practices.    |\\\\n| **Database**          | PostgreSQL (via Amazon Aurora Serverless v2)       | Reliable, ACID compliant, and cost-effective auto-scaling. |\\\\n| **Data Access Layer** | Prisma ORM                                         | Type-safe, simplifies database interactions.               |\\\\n| **Infrastructure**    | AWS Serverless: Lambda, API Gateway, S3, CloudFront| Pay-per-use, highly scalable, managed services.            |\\\\n| **Authentication**    | Custom JWT-based solution within NestJS            | Maximum flexibility for the required invite-only flow.     |\\\\n| **Content Management**| Strapi (Headless CMS, self-hosted)                 | User-friendly for non-technical editors, rapid setup.      |\\\\n| **CI/CD**             | AWS CodePipeline, AWS CDK (TypeScript for IaC)     | Full automation of testing and deployment.                 |\\\\n\\\\n### 3. Execution Phases\\\\n\\\\n**Phase 1: Foundational Setup (Infrastructure & Backend)**\\\\n1.  **Infrastructure:** Provision the core AWS environment using AWS CDK.\\\\n    - Create a new VPC with public and private subnets across multiple Availability Zones.\\\\n    - Deploy the Amazon Aurora Serverless v2 PostgreSQL database into private subnets.\\\\n    - Set up AWS Secrets Manager for storing database credentials.\\\\n    - Configure initial IAM roles and policies with least-privilege access.\\\\n2.  **Backend:** Develop the core application and authentication.\\\\n    - Initialize the NestJS project with all required dependencies.\\\\n    - Establish the Prisma schema for `User` and other core models.\\\\n    - Implement the secure, invite-only user registration flow.\\\\n    - Implement the JWT-based login/logout/refresh endpoints.\\\\n    - Create Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) guards (`resident`, `admin`).\\\\n\\\\n**Phase 2: Content & Frontend Shell (Content Management & Frontend)**\\\\n1.  **Content Management:** Deploy and configure the Strapi CMS.\\\\n    - Provision a small, cost-effective host for the Strapi Docker container.\\\\n    - Define the 'News & Events' content type within the Strapi admin panel.\\\\n    - Configure public API access for published content.\\\\n    - Set up a webhook to be triggered upon content changes.\\\\n2.  **Frontend:** Initialize the project and build the main user interface structure.\\\\n    - Initialize the React project using Vite.\\\\n    - Build the primary `AppShell` component (Header, Footer, Navigation).\\\\n    - Implement client-side routing using React Router, including `AuthGuard` components for protected routes.\\\\n    - Connect the frontend to the backend's authentication endpoints (Login, Logout). Implement global state management for user authentication status.\\\\n\\\\n**Phase 3: Feature Implementation & Integration (All Teams)**\\\\n1.  **Backend:** Build out all required API endpoints as defined in the plan (e.g., for documents, user profiles, etc.).\\\\n2.  **Frontend:** Build all individual pages.\\\\n    - **Public Pages:** 'Contact Us', 'Bylaws', 'Schools'. Fetch data from the backend API where necessary.\\\\n    - **Protected Pages:** 'Dues', 'Annual Meetings'. These pages will be wrapped by the `AuthGuard`.\\\\n    - **News & Events Page:** Fetch and display content from the Strapi API.\\\\n3.  **Integration:** Connect all frontend components to their corresponding backend and Strapi API endpoints.\\\\n\\\\n**Phase 4: Deployment, Testing & Handoff**\\\\n1.  **Infrastructure:** Finalize and deploy the full CI/CD pipeline in AWS CodePipeline.\\\\n    - The pipeline will automatically test and deploy the backend to AWS Lambda and the frontend to S3/CloudFront.\\\\n    - Configure the production API Gateway to use a Lambda authorizer for validating JWTs from the backend.\\\\n2.  **Testing:** Conduct comprehensive end-to-end testing of all user flows, including registration, login, accessing protected content, and form submissions.\\\\n3.  **Handoff:** Create a simple user guide for the non-technical administrator on how to use the Strapi CMS to add and update 'News & Events' articles. Deliver the final, deployed website.\\\"\\n}\\n###<20240725_1819_Store_DEP>###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-25T18:19:04.831Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 1\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"<20240725_1819_Store_DEP>\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Document successfully stored. Your unique storage ID is: DOC-000001\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-25T18:19:04.831Z###\"}]}],\"config\":{},\"tools\":[],\"output\":{}}","genkit:output":"{\"candidates\":[{\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"The Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) is now complete and has been stored in the Company File System under the ID `DOC-000001`. My leadership team has provided excellent, detailed plans, which have been harmonized into a single, actionable strategy.\\n\\nWith the planning phase concluded, we are ready to begin building the HOA website. I will now request to move to the Exe